<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Patan, Gujarat</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Anahilavada, Anahilapataka, Anhilwara, Anhilwad Patan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Patan is a city in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, known in medieval times as Anahilavada or Anahilapataka. Founded by tradition around 746 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vanraj-chavda">Vanaraja</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chavda-dynasty">Chavda dynasty</a>, it was the capital of Gujarat under the Chaulukyas and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghel">Vaghelas</a> until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alauddin-khalji">Alauddin Khalji</a>'s conquest at the end of the 13th century. Its Rani ki Vav stepwell is a World Heritage Site, and the city is known for patola <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> weaving.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/patan-gujarat.jpg" alt="Patan, Gujarat" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bernard Gagnon · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rani_ki_vav_02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
